35:["$","p","15",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"To understand why supply and demand zones work so reliably, you must understand the players behind the market. Retail traders like you and me typically trade with accounts ranging from $500 to $50,000. But institutional traders — investment banks, central banks, hedge funds, and proprietary trading firms — enter trades worth hundreds of millions to billions of dollars."}]}]
36:["$","p","16",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"Due to the sheer size of these institutional orders, they cannot be filled instantly at a single price. Instead, large institutions leave pending orders at specific price zones. When price returns to those zones, those pending orders get triggered again — causing the same explosive price movement that created the zone in the first place."}]}]
37:["$","p","17",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"This is why supply and demand zones are sometimes called “order blocks” in Smart Money Concept (SMC) trading. By identifying these zones on your chart, you are essentially reading the footprints of institutional players and aligning your trades with theirs."}]}]
38:["$","h1","18",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"How to Identify Supply and Demand Zones: 3 Methods"}]}]
39:["$","p","19",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"There are three reliable methods to spot valid supply and demand zones on any chart or timeframe. Use any one of these methods, or combine all three for higher-confidence zones."}]}]
3a:["$","h2","20",{"id":"h2-method-1-momentum-candles-most-reliable-3","children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"Method 1: Momentum Candles (Most Reliable)"}]}]
3b:["$","p","21",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"children":"Step 1 – Spot the momentum candles."}]," Look for a sequence of at least 3 large, consecutive candles all moving in the same direction (all bullish or all bearish). These are called momentum candles and represent institutional participation. Small candles do not qualify — they represent retail activity with no real liquidity behind them."]}]}]
3c:["$","p","22",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"children":"Step 2 – Locate where the move started."}]," Scroll back to the candle that appeared just before the momentum sequence began. This is your “base candle” — the candle where institutional orders were originally placed."]}]}]
3d:["$","p","23",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"children":"Step 3 – Draw your zone rectangle."}]," Using your charting platform’s rectangle tool, draw a box from the high to the low of that base candle and extend it to the right across your chart. This rectangular zone is your supply or demand zone."]}]}]
3e:["$","p","24",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"children":["$","em",null,{"children":"Pro tip:"}]}]," If the zone has already been retested once and price bounced away from it, this retest serves as extra confirmation that the zone is valid and still active."]}]}]
3f:["$","h2","25",{"id":"h2-method-2-consolidation-zones-4","children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"Method 2: Consolidation Zones"}]}]
40:["$","p","26",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"Consolidation happens when the market moves sideways in a tight range before breaking out in a strong direction. This sideways range is where institutions are quietly accumulating or distributing their positions. To use this method, identify the consolidation range, draw a rectangle across the entire sideways area, and treat this rectangle as your supply or demand zone. When price returns to this area after the breakout, expect a powerful continuation of the original direction."}]}]
41:["$","h2","27",{"id":"h2-method-3-concentration-of-wicks-5","children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"Method 3: Concentration of Wicks"}]}]
42:["$","p","28",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"Candlestick wicks (also called shadows) represent price rejection. When you see multiple candles with long wicks all clustering at the same price level, it means price is repeatedly being rejected from that area — a strong sign that significant buy or sell orders are sitting there. Draw a rectangle across the area where the wicks concentrate. This wick cluster acts as your supply or demand zone. When price returns and fails to break through again, it is a high-quality trade signal."}]}]
43:["$","h1","29",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":"How to Trade Supply and Demand Zones: Step-by-Step"}]}]
44:["$","p","30",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"children":"Step 1: Identify the zone."}]," Use one of the three methods above to find and draw a valid supply or demand zone on your chart."]}]}]
45:["$","p","31",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"children":"Step 2: Wait for price to return."}]," Do not enter a trade just because you identified a zone. Wait for price to actually come back and retest that zone."]}]}]
46:["$","p","32",{"children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"rgb(0, 0, 0)"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"children":"Step 3: Wait for confirmation."}]," This is the most important step. When price enters your zone, do NOT enter immediately. Watch for a confirmation signal such as:"]}]}]
